r those  who need additional support they're back  to back so teens usually get here  around one and then they get picked up  around nine which is what they and  their families found ideal the teen  talks program is really  heavily geared towards youth mental  health which is especially important  right now in pandemic times  with rising rates of youth depression  and unfortunately youth  suicides so teen talks partners which  with bridge counseling associates one of  the  premier nonprofit mental health  organizations here  in southern nevada they send out staff  to lead  structured but open discussions on  topics that the teens  want to talk about so it's a safe space  for them to have productive  conversations on topics like  peer pressure and substance abuse and  yes even  depression and suicide then they move  into  the teen connection program right after  and teen connection is all about service  learning  and life skill development so the teens  engage in a wide range  of volunteerism projects within the  community be that  with the nevada animal shelters  with the cancer organizations  cancer groups be that a whole bunch of  different  projects after they combine those  service learning projects  with life skill development  opportunities so they get to learn from  those experiences out in the community  with animal shelters with cancer  organizations and then they mold them  into  lessons that they taught themselves to  inform future projects  and then they bring in outside parties  to teach them  more things so for example the teens a  couple years ago  said that they had they knew somebody  that was hearing impaired  up here and so they wanted to learn sign  language so we brought in somebody  and they spent one of their life skill  development days learning about sign  language  uh later on we have this session we have  somebody coming out  to teach them about money management and  financial literacy  and so they uh they get to to do all  these really cool life skills  development projects  um throughout the session then they go  into teen night which is all social  activity so we do things like  murder mystery nights disco nights all  sorts of these really fun  uh themed experiences where they get to  just kind of let loose  uh and and be kids have fun yeah  wow this sounds like such a  comprehensive  program it really touches on so many  factors of a teenager's life and what  they're concerned about and what they  think is important  um i really like that you guys bring in  the teenagers to talk about these  programs what they want how important is  it to collaborate  with the youth in our area  absolutely it's especially important  youth voice and youth choice are  especially important especially  in these adolescent ages teens have  a lot of complacent options today right  they can stay home and they can play  video games  and many of them lately have been forced  to do that  they can just stick to their  neighborhood or  when they're left unsupervised because  parents have to work and can't afford  child care  they're left to their own volition and  that's what caused the  rise in teen crime and mischief that i  talked about which launched these teen  programs  but to convince them to let go of these  other more complacent opportunities and  and  come to do something that's productive  something that develops  them into tomorrow's community leaders  requires that they are at the helm of  the decision-making process from  building the framework  all the way through execution and so  every component  of all of our team programs so i've  mentioned  three there are several others that we  have the  teens are at the helm from the very  beginning all the way through they pick  the topics for teen talks they pick  their service learning projects they  pick their life skill development  opportunities they pick their themes for  teen night  we have a program called the dreamer lab  in partnership with charlie rocket and  cox communications  at our heinrich facility in our durango  hills facility  those again are spaces where the teens  get to  decide how to create within them so they  come to these  very very unique spaces and there are  really qualified staff that will guide  them of course  but they're at the helm of what they end  up wanting to  grow into that's so great and  for centennial hills for in terms of  that you know crime that was going on  with  the teenagers there why is that is it  because there wasn't  many options for them to you know it's a  growing community they're adding more  things to do but it was it because there  wasn't as much for teenagers to do out  there did you guys ever discuss  that yeah absolutely it's it's simply  because of that that complacency factor  there's a a  common misconception that once my child  is  12 13 they can stay home alone  but unfortunately their brain isn't  fully developed they're still relying on  instinctual behaviors right they're not  thinking through  their decisions from from a to z and so  it's very easy to get into  mischief and then from mischief it's  very easy to get  into criminal behavior and that's  exactly what was happening the  the the wave was dubbed the caught on  camera crimes by teens wave  so it was teens going to houses and  stealing amazon packages  throwing rocks at windows um and and it  just evolved from that  so again it's it's this um something  that the y  is is constantly working on is promoting  the fact that the why is a hub  for youth and adults of all ages all  abilities and especially all income  levels  a common uh barrier to accessing  programs is that financial barrier but  as a non-profit we eliminate that  barrier we will work with whatever  income you have  to make sure we develop either you or  your child into the person  um that reaches their full potential wow  and for people that want to take part in  this for teenagers  um do they have to belong to the  centennial hills ymca 